dollars we don't have.

00:30:53.151 --> 00:30:54.686
And as I explained
in Only The Strong,

00:30:54.686 --> 00:30:57.755 align:start size:88% position:13%
waging war on America's
energy production.

00:30:57.755 --> 00:30:59.190
Those are the two main reasons

00:30:59.190 --> 00:31:01.559
we have record high inflation,

00:31:01.559 --> 00:31:03.094
and why so many
American families

00:31:03.094 --> 00:31:05.997
struggle to make their budgets
meet at the end of the month.

00:31:05.997 --> 00:31:08.600 align:start size:94% position:6%
- So what would you want,
I almost said Obama,

00:31:08.600 --> 00:31:10.835 align:start size:84% position:16%
who might be
pulling the strings.

00:31:10.835 --> 00:31:13.304
What would you want
Biden to do today?

00:31:13.304 --> 00:31:15.673
If you sat down with
him today and said,

00:31:15.673 --> 00:31:18.743 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Hey, this energy crisis,
we have to fix this."

00:31:18.743 --> 00:31:20.445
What would you want
him to do right now?

00:31:20.445 --> 00:31:22.347 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well I write a little bit
about this in Only The Strong,

00:31:22.347 --> 00:31:23.815
because it can't
be stressed enough

00:31:23.815 --> 00:31:25.817 align:start size:84% position:16%
that energy literally
powers America.

00:31:25.817 --> 00:31:27.619
That's why we call it the
power, the power's out,

00:31:27.619 --> 00:31:29.220
oh, the power's back on.

00:31:29.220 --> 00:31:31.122 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's what makes a
difference in modern life

00:31:31.122 --> 00:31:32.624
going back 250 years

00:31:32.624 --> 00:31:36.060 align:start size:97% position:3%
is the energy we get from
sources like coal, oil, gas,

00:31:36.060 --> 00:31:37.996
and now nuclear power as well.

00:31:37.996 --> 00:31:41.099 align:start size:94% position:6%
We have to recognize
that those energy sources

00:31:41.099 --> 00:31:43.534
are gonna power America for
the rest of our lifetime.

00:31:43.534 --> 00:31:46.471
It is a fantasy to think that
wind power and solar power

00:31:46.471 --> 00:31:49.007 align:start size:84% position:16%
is somehow going to
replace those sources.

00:31:49.007 --> 00:31:51.042 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, we still get
seven-eighth of our energy

00:31:51.042 --> 00:31:53.978 align:start size:94% position:6%
from fossil fuels and
nuclear power in America.

00:31:53.978 --> 00:31:56.948
And even among so-called
renewable or clean energy,

00:31:56.948 --> 00:32:00.785 align:start size:94% position:6%
we get a lot more from
water, and wood, and crops

00:32:00.785 --> 00:32:03.288 align:start size:88% position:13%
than we do get from
wind and solar combined.

00:32:03.288 --> 00:32:05.123
So as I write in
Only The Strong,

00:32:05.123 --> 00:32:09.661 align:start position:0%
we have to reinvigorate the
fossil fuel industry in America.

00:32:09.661 --> 00:32:12.931
Now there's no single thing
that Democrats have done

00:32:12.931 --> 00:32:14.966
to strangle fossil fuel
production in America,

00:32:14.966 --> 00:32:18.236
so there's no single thing
we can do to help unleash it.

00:32:18.236 --> 00:32:20.405
But there's a lot of
steps we can take.

00:32:21.873 --> 00:32:24.342 align:start size:88% position:13%
For instance, we can
start building critical
infrastructure,

00:32:24.342 --> 00:32:26.411
most importantly new pipelines.

00:32:26.411 --> 00:32:28.680 align:start size:97% position:3%
I was in Western Pennsylvania
a couple weeks ago

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:30.415
with Mehmet Oz on
the campaign trail.

00:32:30.415 --> 00:32:32.350
We visited a natural gas pad.

00:32:32.350 --> 00:32:35.386
There's enough natural gas
in Pennsylvania, and Ohio,

00:32:35.386 --> 00:32:37.055
and the rest of America

00:32:37.055 --> 00:32:39.724 align:start size:84% position:16%
to essentially replace
all of the energy

00:32:39.724 --> 00:32:41.592
that we import in this country.

00:32:41.592 --> 00:32:45.697 align:start size:97% position:3%
Yet New England is paying
sometimes quadruple the rates

00:32:45.697 --> 00:32:48.266
for natural gas this winter as
the rest of the country does.

00:32:48.266 --> 00:32:49.500
Why?

00:32:49.500 --> 00:32:51.102
Because New York
won't let pipelines

00:32:51.102 --> 00:32:55.340 align:start size:97% position:3%
be built across its territory
to provide American gas

00:32:55.340 --> 00:32:57.241
to American citizens.

00:32:57.241 --> 00:32:59.744
Think about the LNG,
liquified natural gas

00:32:59.744 --> 00:33:01.212
that we could export
to other countries.

00:33:01.212 --> 00:33:05.149 align:start size:97% position:3%
Wouldn't it be better if
Europe was relying on America

00:33:05.149 --> 00:33:09.754 align:start size:97% position:3%
for American gas, rather
than Russia for Russian gas?

00:33:09.754 --> 00:33:11.990
Look at the amount of permitting

00:33:11.990 --> 00:33:14.959 align:start size:84% position:16%
that has not occurred
on federal lands,

00:33:14.959 --> 00:33:17.595 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is almost 30%
of this entire country

00:33:17.595 --> 00:33:21.366 align:start size:97% position:3%
to allow oil and gas
exploration or federal waters.

00:33:21.366 --> 00:33:23.401
So there's a lot of
steps we could take

00:33:23.401 --> 00:33:25.536
to make sure that the
federal government

00:33:25.536 --> 00:33:28.373 align:start size:97% position:3%
is encouraging and
promoting energy production

00:33:28.373 --> 00:33:29.574
in this country.

00:33:29.574 --> 00:33:31.509 align:start size:84% position:16%
Some of those are also
simply rhetorical,

00:33:31.509 --> 00:33:32.977
or even economic.

00:33:32.977 --> 00:33:35.880 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, there are many left
wing banks on Wall Street

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:38.349
that are part of the
Democrats campaign

00:33:38.349 --> 00:33:40.451
against fossil fuels in America.

00:33:40.451 --> 00:33:42.620 align:start size:84% position:16%
We should be
celebrating the fact

00:33:42.620 --> 00:33:45.456 align:start size:88% position:13%
that we have roughnecks
and coal miners

00:33:45.456 --> 00:33:48.393
who are helping achieve
energy independence here,

00:33:48.393 --> 00:33:51.095
and providing energy to our
allies around the world.

00:33:51.095 --> 00:33:52.597 align:start size:84% position:16%
We shouldn't be
trying to cut them off

00:33:52.597 --> 00:33:55.433
from access to the credit
and the equity markets.

00:33:55.433 --> 00:33:57.035
If we take those steps,

00:33:57.035 --> 00:33:59.871 align:start size:84% position:16%
if Biden would simply
appeal to sanity

00:33:59.871 --> 00:34:02.673
when it comes to our energy
sources here in America,

00:34:02.673 --> 00:34:04.075
we could bring gas prices down,

00:34:04.075 --> 00:34:06.544 align:start size:97% position:3%
we could bring your
electricity bills back down,

00:34:06.544 --> 00:34:07.745
and we could help rebuild

00:34:07.745 --> 00:34:09.747
American power around
the world as well.

00:34:09.747 --> 00:34:11.149
- Yeah, unfortunately,

00:34:11.149 --> 00:34:13.484 align:start position:0%
I think I can speak for everyone
watching this right now,

00:34:13.484 --> 00:34:15.386
they're going, they just
know he's not gonna do it.

00:34:15.386 --> 00:34:16.788
Like it's just not in the cards.

00:34:16.788 --> 00:34:18.189 align:start size:84% position:16%
But speaking of
energy and pipelines

00:34:18.189 --> 00:34:19.657
and all of these things,

00:34:19.657 --> 00:34:22.560 align:start size:97% position:3%
do we have any sense of what
happened to Nord Stream?

00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:24.762 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, we basically
just don't talk about it.

00:34:24.762 --> 00:34:27.465
You know, these, I think
it was four explosions.

00:34:27.465 --> 00:34:29.000 align:start size:81% position:19%
We just don't know
what's going on

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:30.535
and we're just letting it be.

00:34:33.938 --> 00:34:35.706 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I probably shouldn't
comment, Dave,

00:34:35.706 --> 00:34:37.708
on what happened in the
Nord Stream pipeline.

00:34:37.708 --> 00:34:39.811
I will say that it never
should have gotten that far

00:34:39.811 --> 00:34:41.079
in the first place.

00:34:41.079 --> 00:34:43.314 align:start size:84% position:16%
Donald Trump was right
to try to stop it

00:34:43.314 --> 00:34:45.016
before it got near conclusion.

00:34:45.016 --> 00:34:47.218
And I'm glad that it's not
gonna be brought online.

00:34:47.218 --> 00:34:49.220 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is something that
Democratic administrations

00:34:49.220 --> 00:34:52.223
before President Trump should
have stopped in its infancy

00:34:52.223 --> 00:34:55.426
so it wasn't able to be used
as leverage against the West.

00:34:56.260 --> 00:34:57.495
- Got it.

00:34:57.495 --> 00:34:59.263
All right, let's shift
a little way from energy

00:34:59.263 --> 00:35:03.835
to what seems to be driving
the Progressives of today,

00:35:03.835 --> 00:35:06.170 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is the woke
stuff, the gender stuff,

00:35:06.170 --> 00:35:07.805
the race stuff, all of that.

00:35:09.140 --> 00:35:10.775
I guess that doesn't
surprise you either.

00:35:10.775 --> 00:35:12.577
Like that was just
gonna be the next

00:35:12.577 --> 00:35:16.681
sort of cultural extension of
the deep state in some sense,

00:35:16.681 --> 00:35:18.082
or the administrative state.

00:35:19.450 --> 00:35:20.618
- It doesn't surprise me.

00:35:20.618 --> 00:35:22.186 align:start size:88% position:13%
Most alarming as I
write in Only The Strong

00:35:22.186 --> 00:35:25.356
is it seeping into
our military as well.

00:35:25.356 --> 00:35:26.891 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is done largely
at the behest

00:35:26.891 --> 00:35:28.159
of the civilian appointees

00:35:28.159 --> 00:35:30.595 align:start size:88% position:13%
at the Department of
Defense under Joe Biden.

00:35:30.595 --> 00:35:33.364 align:start position:0%
The idea that there's a
sexist, or racist, or extremist

00:35:33.364 --> 00:35:34.899
lurking around every corner,

00:35:34.899 --> 00:35:38.669 align:start size:94% position:6%
hiding behind a highly
polished increased veneer,

00:35:38.669 --> 00:35:40.605
which is simply not the case.

00:35:40.605 --> 00:35:43.808
Our military is exceptional.

00:35:43.808 --> 00:35:46.577
They recruit from
a very select group

00:35:46.577 --> 00:35:48.412
of young men and women.

00:35:48.412 --> 00:35:52.250
You're much safer, and you're
much more likely to be judged

00:35:52.250 --> 00:35:55.419 align:start size:97% position:3%
on your merits in our military
than you are, for instance,

00:35:55.419 --> 00:35:58.456 align:start size:94% position:6%
at some left wing college
campus in America.

00:35:59.590 --> 00:36:02.493 align:start size:88% position:13%
That hasn't stopped down
to the lowest level,

00:36:02.493 --> 00:36:04.962 align:start size:88% position:13%
an effort to
indoctrinate our troops

00:36:04.962 --> 00:36:08.533 align:start size:97% position:3%
with things like critical race
theory, or gender ideology.

00:36:08.533 --> 00:36:10.368
I'll tell you a story that
I tell in Only The Strong

00:36:10.368 --> 00:36:11.736
as an example.

00:36:11.736 --> 00:36:13.771
A friend of mine with
whom I served recently

00:36:13.771 --> 00:36:15.206
became a battalion commander.

00:36:15.206 --> 00:36:18.409 align:start size:94% position:6%
So he's in charge of
about 800 to 1,000 troops.

00:36:18.409 --> 00:36:22.613 align:start size:94% position:6%
He has to ensure that
his troops all go through

00:36:22.613 --> 00:36:25.483 align:start size:97% position:3%
something called the
Healthy Sexuality Briefing.

00:36:25.483 --> 00:36:26.984
Now this is a lot more
than what troops got back

00:36:26.984 --> 00:36:28.886
in World War II about
the threats of STD

00:36:28.886 --> 00:36:30.354
and things like that.

00:36:30.354 --> 00:36:33.591 align:start size:97% position:3%
This is all about gender
identity, and transitioning,

00:36:33.591 --> 00:36:35.793
and so on and so forth.

00:36:35.793 --> 00:36:37.862
And he says that this is
tracked all the way up

00:36:37.862 --> 00:36:39.096
to the division headquarters

00:36:39.096 --> 00:36:41.866
and presumably submitted
to some higher command

00:36:41.866 --> 00:36:43.768
at the Department of Defense.

00:36:43.768 --> 00:36:45.703 align:start size:84% position:16%
So he says, "Put
yourself in the shoes

00:36:45.703 --> 00:36:47.004
"of one of my company commanders

00:36:47.004 --> 00:36:50.374 align:start size:88% position:13%
"who's in charge of
about 125 of my troops."

00:36:50.374 --> 00:36:52.643
He knows that he's
got some bureaucrat

00:36:52.643 --> 00:36:55.046
up at higher headquarters
looking over his shoulder

00:36:55.046 --> 00:36:57.181
to ensure that there's a
little green check mark

00:36:57.181 --> 00:36:58.416
next to every soldier's name

00:36:58.416 --> 00:37:01.118 align:start size:84% position:16%
on the Healthy
Sexuality Briefing.

00:37:01.118 --> 00:37:02.853 align:start size:84% position:16%
He doesn't have
that same bureaucrat

00:37:02.853 --> 00:37:04.388
looking over his
shoulder on things

00:37:04.388 --> 00:37:07.692 align:start size:97% position:3%
like shoot, move, and
communicate tactical training

00:37:07.692 --> 00:37:09.060
at the squad level.

00:37:09.060 --> 00:37:10.861 align:start size:84% position:16%
So what is he gonna
take a risk on

00:37:10.861 --> 00:37:12.830
in his quarterly training plan?

00:37:12.830 --> 00:37:15.900
Is he gonna take a risk on
not achieving 100% briefing

00:37:15.900 --> 00:37:17.668
on healthy sexuality,

00:37:17.668 --> 00:37:20.004
or is he gonna take a risk
that not all of his troops

00:37:20.004 --> 00:37:24.275 align:start position:0%
will go through the appropriate
kind of tactical training

00:37:24.275 --> 00:37:26.811
to have them ready to fight
and win on the battlefield?

00:37:26.811 --> 00:37:28.813 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think the question
answers itself.

00:37:28.813 --> 00:37:31.048
And now maybe they
won't go to war,

00:37:31.048 --> 00:37:33.484
and maybe their lives
won't be put at risk,

00:37:33.484 --> 00:37:35.653 align:start size:97% position:3%
because our military
prioritize the wrong things

00:37:35.653 --> 00:37:36.587
in training.

00:37:36.587 --> 00:37:38.256
But do we really want to gamble

00:37:38.256 --> 00:37:39.991
our national security on that?

00:37:39.991 --> 00:37:43.728 align:start size:97% position:3%
More to the point, do we wanna
gamble their safety on it?

00:37:44.562 --> 00:37:46.097
I suggest not.

00:37:46.097 --> 00:37:48.532
All this can end in a stroke,
but it takes a new president,

00:37:48.532 --> 00:37:51.002 align:start size:94% position:6%
a new Secretary of Defense
to put an end to it.

00:37:51.002 --> 00:37:52.403
- So is that what it would take?

00:37:52.403 --> 00:37:53.804
I mean literally, so if we got,

00:37:53.804 --> 00:37:55.306 align:start size:84% position:16%
let's say a
Republican president,

00:37:55.306 --> 00:37:57.642
we now have a different
Secretary of Defense.

00:37:57.642 --> 00:37:59.377 align:start size:81% position:19%
I mean, in essence
you're saying,

00:37:59.377 --> 00:38:01.012
you have to fire a
tremendous amount

00:38:01.012 --> 00:38:03.114
of the top brass at
the military, right?

00:38:03.114 --> 00:38:04.982 align:start size:88% position:13%
- No, I don't think
that's necessary, Dave,

00:38:04.982 --> 00:38:06.517
because I mean, the
military is different

00:38:06.517 --> 00:38:08.052 align:start size:84% position:16%
from say the
Department of Labor,

00:38:08.052 --> 00:38:10.888 align:start size:94% position:6%
or the Department of
Health and Human Services.

00:38:10.888 --> 00:38:13.758 align:start size:97% position:3%
There is a chain of command,
and there's an ethos

00:38:13.758 --> 00:38:17.762
of moving out when you've
received a lawful order.

00:38:19.163 --> 00:38:20.531 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think it would only
take a new president,

00:38:20.531 --> 00:38:22.433 align:start position:0%
Republican president,
Republican Secretary of Defense,

00:38:22.433 --> 00:38:24.135
to say all this ends today.

00:38:24.135 --> 00:38:27.204
So for instance, Dave, when I
was in the army, I frequently,

00:38:27.204 --> 00:38:29.507 align:start position:0%
well I shouldn't say frequently,
once every six months,

00:38:29.507 --> 00:38:31.909 align:start size:84% position:16%
we had a lot of
mandatory briefings.

00:38:31.909 --> 00:38:33.878
You normally set aside a
morning of training for it,

00:38:33.878 --> 00:38:37.281
and it was on everything from
equal opportunity policies,

00:38:37.281 --> 00:38:41.252
to motorcycle safety,
to personal finances.

00:38:41.252 --> 00:38:44.689
And that briefing made
clear, albeit shortly,

00:38:44.689 --> 00:38:48.726
that the army is an equal
opportunity organization.

00:38:48.726 --> 00:38:49.927
It does not tolerate

00:38:49.927 --> 00:38:51.829 align:start size:84% position:16%
any kind of harassment
or discrimination

00:38:51.829 --> 00:38:55.733
on the basis of race, or sex,
or religion, or nationality,

00:38:55.733 --> 00:38:56.867
or what have you.

00:38:56.867 --> 00:38:58.102
And that was that.

00:38:58.102 --> 00:38:59.704 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I never saw
anything like that

00:38:59.704 --> 00:39:01.772
when I was in the army, Dave.

00:39:01.772 --> 00:39:04.575 align:start size:97% position:3%
All it takes is one
Secretary of Defense to say,

00:39:04.575 --> 00:39:08.412
"We're going back to the old
ways of reminding our troops

00:39:08.412 --> 00:39:11.482
"that this is an equal
employment organization,

00:39:11.482 --> 00:39:12.917
"equal opportunity organization,

00:39:12.917 --> 00:39:14.385
"and we have zero tolerance

00:39:14.385 --> 00:39:16.654 align:start size:97% position:3%
"for any kind of
harassment discrimination."

00:39:16.654 --> 00:39:20.057
Here's one PowerPoint slide
that states that policy.

00:39:20.057 --> 00:39:21.759 align:start size:94% position:6%
A soldier will get briefed
on that once a year

00:39:21.759 --> 00:39:23.427
or once every six months.

00:39:23.427 --> 00:39:26.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
And if I find out any
command is using anything

00:39:26.130 --> 00:39:29.066
other than this single
statement of policy,

00:39:29.066 --> 00:39:31.469
that commander will be removed.

00:39:31.469 --> 00:39:33.070 align:start size:84% position:16%
That will end the
nonsense right there,

00:39:33.070 --> 00:39:34.605
I promise you.

00:39:34.605 --> 00:39:36.607
Because that's what most of
these commanders want to do.

00:39:36.607 --> 00:39:40.311
You think my friend, who just
became a battalion commander,

00:39:40.311 --> 00:39:43.147
who's achieving the pinnacle
of his professional career,

00:39:43.147 --> 00:39:45.316
wants to spend his time on a
healthy sexuality briefing?

00:39:45.316 --> 00:39:50.221 align:start size:84% position:16%
No, the vast majority
of officers and
senior enlisted troops

00:39:50.221 --> 00:39:52.490 align:start size:84% position:16%
want to focus on
training their troops,

00:39:52.490 --> 00:39:53.958
being ready to fight a war.

00:39:53.958 --> 00:39:57.428 align:start size:97% position:3%
They don't wanna have to focus
on all these distractions.

00:39:57.428 --> 00:39:59.630
- When you're back
home in Arkansas,

00:39:59.630 --> 00:40:01.165
and you're talking
to constituents,

00:40:01.165 --> 00:40:03.567
and they're seeing
all of this craziness

00:40:03.567 --> 00:40:05.202
with the gender stuff,

00:40:05.202 --> 00:40:08.906
and we just covered it on my
show where the president is,

00:40:08.906 --> 00:40:10.741
you know, sitting
at the White House

00:40:10.741 --> 00:40:14.845 align:start size:88% position:13%
with a trans journalist
who's, you know,

00:40:14.845 --> 00:40:16.580 align:start size:84% position:16%
and in essence he's
saying that the states

00:40:16.580 --> 00:40:18.115
shouldn't have rights

00:40:18.115 --> 00:40:20.317
when it comes to whether these
procedures should go through,

00:40:20.317 --> 00:40:22.186 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, just the
litany of crazy things.

00:40:22.186 --> 00:40:24.655
I would suspect in a
place like Arkansas,

00:40:24.655 --> 00:40:28.993 align:start size:97% position:3%
there is very little tolerance
for this kind of thing.

00:40:30.394 --> 00:40:33.531 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, Dave, I've learned
over the last 10 years

00:40:33.531 --> 00:40:35.032
what I kind of sense growing up

00:40:35.032 --> 00:40:38.369
is you can never underestimate
the good decent common sense

00:40:38.369 --> 00:40:40.271
of hardworking Arkansans.

00:40:40.271 --> 00:40:45.075
And when they hear things
like kids being exposed

00:40:45.075 --> 00:40:49.447
to transgender ideology in
kindergarten or second grade,

00:40:49.447 --> 00:40:52.650
of doctors performing
double mastectomies

00:40:52.650 --> 00:40:54.185
on healthy teenage girls,

00:40:54.185 --> 00:40:59.156 align:start size:97% position:3%
or using drugs to block
normal pubescent development

00:41:00.391 --> 00:41:01.659
in boys and girls, they
just think it's insane.

00:41:01.659 --> 00:41:03.661
Our legislature has
in fact taken action

00:41:03.661 --> 00:41:05.996
to stop that kind of
abuse of children,

00:41:05.996 --> 00:41:09.266
which I think any decent
civilized society should.

00:41:09.266 --> 00:41:11.669 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's one thing when someone
becomes 18 years old

00:41:11.669 --> 00:41:14.538 align:start size:97% position:3%
and is an adult and can make
decisions for themselves,

00:41:14.538 --> 00:41:16.774
but just like we take
a lot of other steps

00:41:16.774 --> 00:41:21.779 align:start size:88% position:13%
to protect children
from fads, and fashions,

00:41:22.646 --> 00:41:24.381
and from irresponsible adults,

00:41:24.381 --> 00:41:27.084 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think this is a reasonable
step that we take as well

00:41:27.084 --> 00:41:29.220 align:start size:94% position:6%
in my state and hopefully
a lot of other states

00:41:29.220 --> 00:41:30.588
to protect young children.

00:41:31.722 --> 00:41:33.224 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Do you sense
that the direction

00:41:33.224 --> 00:41:35.326
that America's going
in where the states

00:41:35.326 --> 00:41:38.929
are now feeling very, very
different than each other?

00:41:38.929 --> 00:41:41.232 align:start size:84% position:16%
I know you appreciate
federalism,

00:41:41.232 --> 00:41:44.168
so, you know, look,
I was in California,

00:41:44.168 --> 00:41:46.337
it went off the deep end,
I now live in Florida.

00:41:46.337 --> 00:41:47.838
As I tell everyone,

00:41:47.838 --> 00:41:49.406 align:start size:97% position:3%
I feel like I live in a
different country actually.

00:41:49.406 --> 00:41:50.841
But do you think that this trend

00:41:50.841 --> 00:41:53.744 align:start size:97% position:3%
where the red states
seemingly will be more red,

00:41:53.744 --> 00:41:56.847 align:start size:94% position:6%
the blue states seemingly
will be more blue,

00:41:56.847 --> 00:41:58.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
do you think there's
a danger there

00:41:58.382 --> 00:42:01.385 align:start size:97% position:3%
that we will have nothing that
will unite us at some point

00:42:01.385 --> 00:42:03.020
if we keep going
down that route?

00:42:04.588 --> 00:42:06.590
- Well Dave, I think
the real danger is

00:42:06.590 --> 00:42:08.292 align:start size:84% position:16%
more of these liberal
states and cities

00:42:08.292 --> 00:42:11.795
trying to impose their
views and their values

00:42:11.795 --> 00:42:14.832 align:start size:88% position:13%
on the more Conservative
areas of our country.

00:42:14.832 --> 00:42:17.701 align:start size:88% position:13%
Take California and
Arkansas as an example.

00:42:17.701 --> 00:42:18.969
I love California.

00:42:18.969 --> 00:42:20.838
It is a beautiful state.

00:42:20.838 --> 00:42:22.940
It's got a great history,
it's got great people.

00:42:22.940 --> 00:42:26.443
I don't agree with much of
the political turn it's taken

00:42:26.443 --> 00:42:28.279
over the last 20 years, however.

00:42:28.279 --> 00:42:30.047
It's lost a lot of
great people like you

00:42:30.047 --> 00:42:31.549
who want to get away
from the higher taxes,

00:42:31.549 --> 00:42:33.250
and the regulation, and
the lack of housing,

00:42:33.250 --> 00:42:34.485
and the high costs.

00:42:35.386 --> 00:42:36.921
At the same time,

00:42:36.921 --> 00:42:39.557
I wouldn't presume to set
the laws from California,

00:42:39.557 --> 00:42:40.925
from Arkansas.

00:42:40.925 --> 00:42:43.227
I wouldn't think
that I get a say,

00:42:43.227 --> 00:42:45.195
or that my fellow
Arkansans get a say

00:42:45.195 --> 00:42:47.498 align:start size:97% position:3%
in dictating what traditional
state and local matters

00:42:47.498 --> 00:42:49.567
to a state like California.

00:42:49.567 --> 00:42:51.068
That's not the case the
other way around though.

00:42:51.068 --> 00:42:53.437
You know, California,
like other states,

00:42:53.437 --> 00:42:56.307 align:start size:94% position:6%
have tried to impose their
views and their values

00:42:56.307 --> 00:42:57.808
on other states.

00:42:57.808 --> 00:42:59.476
Sometimes they even do things
like adopting travel bans,

00:42:59.476 --> 00:43:01.011
saying that California officials

00:43:01.011 --> 00:43:04.048
can't travel to a state
like Texas or Arkansas,

00:43:04.048 --> 00:43:05.683 align:start size:84% position:16%
unless of course
you're Gavin Newsom,

00:43:05.683 --> 00:43:08.452 align:start size:97% position:3%
then you can travel to
places like Idaho and Montana

00:43:08.452 --> 00:43:11.255 align:start size:94% position:6%
to go to your multimillion
dollar resort lodges

00:43:11.255 --> 00:43:13.157
with your security detail.

00:43:13.157 --> 00:43:15.626 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I think federalism
is not just a way

00:43:15.626 --> 00:43:17.061
to protect our liberties

00:43:17.061 --> 00:43:18.729 align:start size:84% position:16%
as our founding
fathers understood it,

00:43:18.729 --> 00:43:23.233
it's also a way to account for
the large continental nation

00:43:23.233 --> 00:43:27.171
with a genuine kind of
diversity, economic,

00:43:27.171 --> 00:43:29.807
geographic, historic diversity

00:43:29.807 --> 00:43:32.042
that allows people
to govern themselves

00:43:32.042 --> 00:43:34.478
in the ways best suited
to their environments.

00:43:34.478 --> 00:43:36.180
You know, Maine for instance,

00:43:36.180 --> 00:43:39.116 align:start size:97% position:3%
is gonna have a lot of
interest and a lot of focus

00:43:39.116 --> 00:43:40.751
on the lobster trade.

00:43:40.751 --> 00:43:44.121
Not exactly something we spend
a lot of time on in Arkansas.

00:43:44.121 --> 00:43:44.922
- Right.

00:43:44.922 --> 00:43:46.123
- But then again,

00:43:46.123 --> 00:43:47.658 align:start size:88% position:13%
Arkansas has a very
large forester industry,

00:43:47.658 --> 00:43:48.993 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, not again
something that you see

00:43:48.993 --> 00:43:50.594
in the great plain states.

00:43:50.594 --> 00:43:55.199
These views extend to social
and cultural questions as well.

00:43:55.199 --> 00:43:57.701
And I think it's fine for states

00:43:57.701 --> 00:43:59.670 align:start size:84% position:16%
to have very
different approaches

00:43:59.670 --> 00:44:01.605 align:start size:84% position:16%
to the way their
citizens want to live.

00:44:01.605 --> 00:44:04.642
It's not fine when some
of these left wing states

00:44:04.642 --> 00:44:07.344 align:start size:84% position:16%
try to then begin
imposing those views

00:44:07.344 --> 00:44:10.547
and those values on
more Conservative
areas of the country.

00:44:10.547 --> 00:44:12.282 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, I think that's
the great challenge

00:44:12.282 --> 00:44:13.350
we have going forward

00:44:13.350 --> 00:44:14.652
is that a certain set of people

00:44:14.652 --> 00:44:16.153
are gonna wanna be left alone,

00:44:16.153 --> 00:44:18.022 align:start size:97% position:3%
and a certain set of people
won't want 'em to leave alone.

00:44:18.022 --> 00:44:21.091
And I don't know how you
arbitrage those two things.

00:44:21.091 --> 00:44:23.527
What, just away from the
book for just a moment,

00:44:23.527 --> 00:44:25.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
what would you want people
to know about Arkansas

00:44:25.362 --> 00:44:26.964
that maybe they don't know?

00:44:26.964 --> 00:44:28.766
You know, we talk
a lot about Cali,

00:44:28.766 --> 00:44:30.668 align:start size:84% position:16%
now we talk about
Florida, or New York,

00:44:30.668 --> 00:44:32.836 align:start size:94% position:6%
or some of the bigger
states or coastal states.

00:44:32.836 --> 00:44:34.738
We don't talk about
Arkansas that much.

00:44:34.738 --> 00:44:37.441
What should people
know about Arkansas?

00:44:37.441 --> 00:44:39.376 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Arkansas is a
great, beautiful state

00:44:39.376 --> 00:44:40.878
with wonderful people.

00:44:40.878 --> 00:44:43.013
You know, if you don't like
so much heat and humidity

00:44:43.013 --> 00:44:44.281 align:start size:81% position:19%
in the summers you
get in Florida,

00:44:44.281 --> 00:44:45.549
you should come to Arkansas

00:44:45.549 --> 00:44:47.151
where we have four
beautiful seasons.

00:44:48.519 --> 00:44:50.554 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's a lot of opportunity
all across the state,

00:44:50.554 --> 00:44:53.824 align:start size:84% position:16%
a real rich diversity
across the state.

00:44:53.824 --> 00:44:55.225
You know, in Eastern Arkansas

00:44:55.225 --> 00:44:58.228 align:start position:0%
we have some of the most
fertile farmland in the country.

00:44:58.228 --> 00:45:02.499 align:start position:0%
South and West Arkansas has
some of the great timber forests

00:45:02.499 --> 00:45:04.001
that we have across the country.

00:45:04.001 --> 00:45:06.837 align:start size:97% position:3%
Beautiful mountains, both
the Ozarks and the Washitas,

00:45:06.837 --> 00:45:08.605
especially in the
Northwest Arkansas,

00:45:08.605 --> 00:45:11.241
there's rapidly growing economy

00:45:11.241 --> 00:45:14.178 align:start size:97% position:3%
that has about 1,000 people
moving in every month.

00:45:14.178 --> 00:45:17.948
So there's just a lot of
rich and rewarding reasons

00:45:17.948 --> 00:45:19.149
to move to Arkansas,

00:45:19.149 --> 00:45:21.251
or at least come and
try it out for a visit.

00:45:21.251 --> 00:45:23.353
There's no bad time
to come to Arkansas.

00:45:23.353 --> 00:45:24.888
We'd welcome you up there, Dave,

00:45:24.888 --> 00:45:27.057
just like we'd welcome a lot
of your California refugees

00:45:27.057 --> 00:45:28.559
coming in as well,

00:45:28.559 --> 00:45:30.828
as long as they don't bring
their politics with them.

00:45:30.828 --> 00:45:33.063
- That seems to be the
key to the entire thing.

00:45:33.063 --> 00:45:36.166 align:start position:0%
I basically have to walk
around here with a DeSantis hat

00:45:36.166 --> 00:45:39.503
to make sure that my neighbors
aren't freaking out about me.

00:45:39.503 --> 00:45:40.871
Senator, I appreciate your time.

00:45:40.871 --> 00:45:42.473
The book is Only The Strong,

00:45:42.473 --> 00:45:44.541
we'll link to it
right down below,

00:45:44.541 --> 00:45:47.811 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I'm gonna be in DC a
little bit after the election

00:45:47.811 --> 00:45:49.012
doing some live interviews,

00:45:49.012 --> 00:45:51.315 align:start size:88% position:13%
so I have a feeling
you'll be in a good mood

00:45:51.315 --> 00:45:52.750
for a couple days after that.

00:45:52.750 --> 00:45:54.885 align:start size:94% position:6%
So if you're in DC, maybe
we can connect then.

00:45:54.885 --> 00:45:56.320
- Thanks Dave.

00:45:56.320 --> 00:45:59.223 align:start position:0%
Swing by in between your meeting
with all the large number

00:45:59.223 --> 00:46:01.725 align:start size:94% position:6%
of new Republican
Senators and Congressmen.

00:46:01.725 --> 00:46:02.993
- Sounds like a plan.

00:46:02.993 --> 00:46:05.162
Senator Tom Cotton,
thank you very much.

